\paragraph{The decision, in one line.} The launch plan fixes only the initial
condition: \emph{when} the plan says each gate should be open, with the cycle,
green, and storage bounds of the timing family. The sole decision variable of
the program is the time-indexed open/closed indicator
$x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\}$ (\cref{eq:x_primary}).  Nothing in the objective is
chosen---only the gate trajectory is---so every reported quantity is a
consequence of that trajectory rather than an input to it.  Action letters,
offsets, cycle lengths, and green splits are \emph{derived} quantities:
they are read off the committed gate schedule and are used by the online
controller of \cref{sec:solutions}; they are \emph{not} variables of this
program.  This is deliberate: in a clairvoyant program the gate schedule is the
decision, and an action vocabulary is a language for proposing schedules, not
a decision space.

\paragraph{Integer time.} Time is integer-indexed throughout this section.
The step is fixed to $\Delta t = 1$\,s and the horizon is
$\Tsp = \{0,1,\dots,T\} \subset \Z_{\ge 0}$; every duration, green, travel
time, and arrival clock is an integer number of steps (seconds).  No
continuous time variable appears in the exact program.

\paragraph{What is solved here, and what is not.} This program answers one
question: if every arrival, queue, and bus timetable were known in advance
for the whole horizon, which signal should show green at every intersection
at every second so that total passenger delay is minimised and every bus
meets its timetable?  The inputs are the street state second by second:
which signal aspect is active, how long each queue is and how fast it
discharges, where each intersection sits in its cycle relative to its
neighbours, where each bus is against its timetable, and how passenger delay
accumulates.  The outputs are the green or not-green choice for every phase
at every second, plus the record of when each bus arrives against its
timetable.  The program does not estimate, learn, or search; it does not
contain detectors, triggers, cooldowns, spillback gates, congestion vetoes,
Q-values, Harmony Search improvisation, Nash utilities, measured feeds, or
the $Z_1$-selection logic.  Those belong to the solution algorithm
(\cref{sec:solutions,sec:selection_layer}); none of them appears as a
constraint of this program.  The deployed code never solves this program as
a whole; at each bus detection it solves one small greedy problem for one
junction and one action (the delay-based learners approximate a single Bellman
stage, while Nash bargaining, max-pressure, and the centralised rules pursue
their own objectives under the same guard protocol; see \cref{tab:methods}).

\subsection{What the Program Computes, Second by Second}
\label{sec:sched_interp}

The program is best read as a second-by-second timetable over the network.
Time is the integer grid $\Tsp = \{0,1,\dots,T\}$ with
$\Delta t = 1$\,s.  What changes per time step is the \emph{operation}: for
every intersection $i$ and every integer step $t$ the schedule specifies
which gate is open, $x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\}$ (at most one phase open; none
open during intergreen clearance).  The optimizer therefore chooses
\emph{when} each operation switches, \emph{how long} each stays open, and
\emph{how} the open steps propagate to neighbouring intersections through
the lagged overlap constraints.  Each row below states one piece of that
timetable in words and points to where the program computes it:

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Piece of the timetable & Where the program computes it \\
\hline
Places being controlled & Intersections $i \in \Isp$ \\
Per-step operation (what changes per $t$) & Open gate $x_{i,p,t}$ / green (\cref{eq:gate_green}) \\
One green at a time (per intersection) & At most one open gate per $i,t$; cycle/green bounds (\cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}) \\
Earliest possible times & Bus entry events $\tau_b^{\text{detect}}$ and free-flow lags $\ell_{ij}$ (\cref{eq:arr_prop}) \\
Ordering across intersections & Lagged main-green overlap (\cref{eq:band_min}) \\
Buses with timetables & Buses with timetable arrivals $\bar{\tau}_{b,i}$ (\cref{eq:arr_prop,eq:sched_cap}) \\
Objective & Minimum total passenger delay, every bus on schedule \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

