\subsection{Equivalent Markovian Formulation: the MIP as a Solved Markov Process}
\label{sec:markovian}

The time-expanded schedule of \cref{sec:sched_interp} can equally be
read as the solution of a \emph{finite-horizon Markovian decision process}:
at every step the controller observes a state, picks an admissible action,
and the corridor transitions deterministically.  Because every transition in
the program is a deterministic (linear) function, deciding the
whole schedule in advance and deciding it stage-by-stage from the current
state produce the same optimal actions--the MIP is the flattened Bellman
recursion.

\paragraph{State, decision, transition.}
The Markov state at integer step $t$ collects only plant quantities--no
sensor flags, no heuristic thresholds:
\begin{equation}
  X_t = \Bigl(
    \{\,x_{i,p,t-1},\, Q_{i,m,v,t}\,\}_{i,p,m,v},\;
    \{\,\tau_{b,i}\,\}_{b \in \Bsp,\, i \in \Rcal_b^{\text{done}}}
  \Bigr)
  \label{eq:markov_state}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ the previous open-gate operations, the predicted queues by movement and
class, and the passage clocks of buses that have already reached an
intersection on their route (empty when $\Bsp = \emptyset$).  Auxiliary
variables ($d$, $Q^w$, $D$, $h$, $\sigma^\pm$, $e$) are deterministic
outputs of $(X_t,u_t)$ held by equalities; they are not state.  The control
at step $t$ is $u_t = \{x_{i,p,t}\}_{i,p}$, and the plant evolves through
the per-step recursions defined in this section:
\begin{equation}
  X_{t+1} = F(X_t, u_t)
  \label{eq:markov_trans}
\end{equation}
where $F$ bundles the queue conservation (\cref{eq:mv_q}), the discharge
definition (\cref{eq:mv_dcap}), the waiting rule (\cref{eq:mv_wait}), and the
bus-clock propagation (\cref{eq:arr_prop}).  $X_{t+1}$ depends on
$(X_t, u_t)$ only--the Markov property holds by construction, which is
precisely why the time-expanded grid needs no additional history variables.

\paragraph{Bellman recursion and exact equivalence.}
The stage cost is the \emph{predicted} delay incurred in step $t$--the
passenger-weighted waiting of every queued vehicle (zero buses = zero bus
terms):
\begin{equation}
  c_t(X,u) \;=\; \sum_{i \in \Isp} \sum_{s \in \Ssp} \sum_{v \in \Vsp}
    \rho_v\, Q^w_{i,s,v,t}(X,u)
  \label{eq:stage_cost}
\end{equation}
with $Q^w_{i,s,v,t} = \sum_{m \in \Msp_i(s,v)} Q^w_{i,m,v,t}$.  The optimal
value function is defined by the Bellman optimality equation
\begin{equation}
  V_t(X) = \min_{u \in \Ucal(X)}
    \Bigl[\, c_t(X, u) + V_{t+1}\!\bigl(F(X, u)\bigr) \Bigr],
  \qquad V_{T+1} \equiv 0
  \label{eq:bellman}
\end{equation}
where $\Ucal(X)$ is physical feasibility only (at most one open gate,
intergreen, minimum green, green/cycle bounds, finite storage).  Unrolling
\cref{eq:bellman} over the horizon reproduces the time-expanded MIP
exactly: the binary gate variables $x_{i,p,t}$ enumerate the minimisation
over $u_t$, the physical-system constraints implement $\Ucal(X_t)$, the
transition equations implement $F$, and the additive objective implements
the sum of stage costs.  Hence
\begin{center}
\emph{solving the MIP $\;\equiv\;$ solving the Markovian process to
optimality with perfect state information;}
\end{center}
there is no optimality gap between the two views.

\begin{theorem}[MILP--Bellman equivalence]
\label{thm:milp_bellman}
Let $x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t)$ be the deterministic transition defined by the
queue conservation (\cref{eq:mv_q}), the discharge definition
(\cref{eq:mv_dcap}), the waiting rule (\cref{eq:mv_wait}), and the
bus-arrival propagation (\cref{eq:arr_prop}, vacuous when $\Bsp=\emptyset$),
with stage cost $c_t$ equal to the passenger-delay term of
\cref{eq:stage_cost}.  Then the finite-horizon deterministic problem
$\min_{u_0,\dots,u_T}\sum_{t=0}^{T}c_t(X_t,u_t)$ subject to the plant
constraints has the same optimal value and the same optimal solution set as
the MILP of \cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:sched_cap} with criterion
\cref{eq:opt_crit} (plant-only form): $V_0(X_0)=Z^*$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Step 1: state and Markov property.} Every quantity that propagates
through time is contained in $X_t$ of \cref{eq:markov_state}: queues
$Q_{i,m,v,t}$, previous operations $x_{i,p,t-1}$, and bus clocks
$\tau_{b,i}$ (empty when $\Bsp=\emptyset$).  The update of each block uses
only $(X_t,u_t)$, so the process is Markov by construction and needs no
history variables.

\emph{Step 2: determinism.} All transitions listed above are deterministic
linear functions: given $(X_t,u_t)$ there is exactly one
$X_{t+1}=F(X_t,u_t)$.  No expectation appears.

\emph{Step 3: control correspondence.} Define $\Phi$ from control sequences
to schedules by setting $x_{i,p,t}=u_{i,p,t}$ and deriving $Q$, $D$, $\tau$
from the plant equations; define $\Psi$ from schedules to controls by
projection.  Both maps are bijective because the gate variables are the
decision variables and all other variables are functions of them.

\emph{Step 4: objective equivalence.} The MILP objective is
$Z=\sum_t c_t(X_t,u_t)$ where $c_t$ is implemented by the queue-waiting
bounds (\cref{eq:mv_wait,eq:mv_waitsum}).  The Bellman recursion is
$V_t(X)=\min_u[c_t(X,u)+V_{t+1}(F(X,u))]$, $V_{T+1}\equiv0$, whose repeated
substitution gives $V_0(X_0)=\min_{u_0,\dots,u_T}\sum_t c_t(X_t,u_t)$:
exactly the MILP objective over the bijected feasible sets.

\emph{Step 5: conclusion.} Feasible schedules and feasible policies coincide,
both optimise the identical cumulative cost under identical transitions and
constraints, so the optimal solution sets coincide and $Z^*=V_0(X_0)$.
\qed
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Deterministic equivalent program]
The MILP is the deterministic equivalent program (DEP) of the
finite-horizon MDP defined by state $X_t$, action $u_t$, transition $F$,
and stage cost $c_t$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every admissible policy induces exactly one feasible trajectory and every
feasible trajectory corresponds to one admissible policy
(Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman}, Step~3); hence optimisation over policies
equals optimisation over schedules.  ``Flattened Bellman recursion'' used
elsewhere in this paper means exactly this DEP relationship. \qed
\end{proof}

\subsubsection{Holding Lemma: every auxiliary is fixed by $(X_t,u_t)$}
\label{sec:holding}
Nothing that touches a decision is free-floating.  Every auxiliary
($Q_{i,m,v,t+1}$, $d_{i,m,v,t}$, $Q^w_{i,m,v,t}$, $D_{i,s,v,t}$,
$\tau_{b,i}$, $\sigma^\pm_{b,i}$, $h_{ij,t}$) is uniquely fixed by the
$(X_0,u_0,\dots,u_T)$ trajectory through the equalities of this section;
inequalities marked $(\star)$ are held tight at any optimum (and can be
imposed as equalities without loss of optimality), so no slack can
disconnect a guard from its state input.  Consequently the transition
$F$ needs no extra history because every right-hand side above is already in
$(X_t,u_t)$.

\subsubsection{Cycle, Green, and Offsets as Outcomes of the One Program}
\label{sec:stage_a}

There is no separate design program and no fixed-time plan standing before
the optimiser.  The cycle, the per-phase green, and the offset are
\emph{reported outcomes} of the committed gate schedule, not decisions of a
prior stage: the program chooses the open/closed pattern $x_{i,p,t}$ and the
quantities below are read off it.  What the timing must satisfy is stated
once, as bounds on the open-step counts, by \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}.
A conventional fixed-time plan is the \emph{special case} in which the
program takes no action and the bounds pin the timing to a single point---a
warm start, not a prior stage.

\paragraph{Timing inputs.} For each intersection $i$ and movement
$m \in \Msp_i$, the exogenous arrival flow $n_{i,m,v,t}$ (veh/step, integer),
and for each phase its saturation $\mu_{i,p}$ (\cref{eq:mu_lanes}); for the
bus phase $p_b^i$ the timetable intensity $\vartheta_i$ (buses per cycle)
and the occupancy $\rho_{\text{bus}}$.  These size the bounds.  They are
data: the arrival \emph{count} is absorbed by the queue recursion
(\cref{eq:mv_q}), never by re-optimising the timing against the mean
(\cref{sec:stochastic}).

\paragraph{Cycle and its bounds.} The nominal cycle $C_i$ is a parameter
fixed for the junction, with bounds $C_i^{\min}, C_i^{\max}$ (integer).  The
program may realise any open-step total within
$[C_i^{\min}-L_i,\, C_i^{\max}-L_i]$ per cycle window; a starting value,
used only to initialise the clock and as a warm start, is the Webster value
on the \emph{critical} flow ratio, computed offline and supplied as data
($C_i^{0}$, clamped to the bounds).  When the critical flow ratio is at or
above unity the junction is oversaturated and the program falls back on the
measured queue state.  Integer arithmetic throughout, so the cycle is a whole
number of seconds.

\paragraph{Green splits.} Green is apportioned by the program through the
open indicators $x_{i,p,t}$ subject to \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}; the
split is the count of open steps per phase per window, not a separate
decision.  A conventional Webster split is the warm start
$\bar{x}_{i,p,t}$.

\paragraph{Offsets.} The offset of intersection $i$ in cycle $c$ is defined
as the first integer step $t$ in $W_{i,c}$ with $x_{i,p_b^i,t}=1$ (the
start of the bus-phase green); it is a \emph{reported outcome} of the gate
schedule.  Coordination is enforced directly by the lagged overlap
constraints of \cref{eq:band_min}, not by an offset variable, which removes
the modulo arithmetic and the multiple offset models that previously
coexisted.  A starting value follows from progression---the timetable fixes a
desired running time $\Theta^{\text{ff}}_{ij}$ between consecutive
intersections---but the running value is not fixed.

\paragraph{Lane capacity.} Saturation is additive over the lanes serving a
phase:
\begin{equation}
  \mu_{i,p} \;=\; \sum_{m:\,\Psp_i(m) = p} \;\sum_{\ell \in \Lsp_{i,m}} \mu_{i,\ell},
  \qquad
  \mu_{i,m} \;=\; \sum_{\ell \in \Lsp_{i,m}} \mu_{i,\ell},
  \label{eq:mu_lanes}
\end{equation}
so a phase inherits the capacity of its lanes and the demand of its
movements, and no timing constraint is ever written per lane.

