\subsection{Computational Status: Formulation Solved Nowhere, Benchmark Claimed Nowhere}
\label{sec:milp_status}

The word ``exact'' in this paper means \emph{exact formulation}, never
\emph{exactly solved benchmark}.  No MILP objective value, optimality gap,
solution time, node count, or branch-and-bound statistic is reported in this
paper, because none exists: the full-horizon program was never solved to
optimality on the Kelvin Grove corridor, and no number from it is used to
rank any controller.  All percentage changes in the Abstract and in
\cref{sec:results} are microsimulation outcomes (Aimsun Next,
\texttt{champion\_kg.csv}) measured against the \texttt{NO\_TSP} fixed-time
baseline, not against an MILP optimum.

\paragraph{Why it was not solved.}  At $\Delta t = 1$\,s the scored horizon is
$T = 4500$ steps (1.25\,h) over $|\Isp| = 12$ intersections with
$|\Psp_i| \approx 4$ phases each.  The time-expanded model therefore carries
roughly $|\Isp|\times|\Psp_i|\times T \approx 2.2\times10^5$ binary gate
variables $x_{i,p,t}$, plus of order $10^5$ integer queue, discharge,
delay, progression-overlap, and bus-clock variables, subject to of order
$10^6$ constraints.  This is two orders of magnitude beyond real-time exact
solution on a 12-junction arterial.  We report the program's scale and our
non-solve explicitly instead.  (The program is smaller than earlier drafts of
this formulation because the action layer, the offset state, the trigger layer,
the Purdue state, and the lane-group block have been removed; the online
controller of \cref{sec:solutions} retains them.)

\paragraph{What the MILP is then for.}  The program is the normative
definition of the control problem (what \emph{should} be done at every
intersection in every second under perfect prediction) and the common parent of
the Bellman recursion (Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman} below) and the
single-event greedy subproblem the delay-based arms approximate
(\cref{sec:appendix_pseudo}); the online guard set and the rule layer
that inherit its physics inherit it through that subproblem rather than by
constraining it.  Its value is definitional and architectural--one
consistent model, one criterion, one rule philosophy from which the
delay-based arms inherit and against which all fifteen scored arms are
measured on the common microsimulation criterion--not numerical.

\subsection{Constraints}
\label{sec:milp_constraints}

\paragraph{Index conventions.} Throughout \cref{sec:milp}: $i,j \in \Isp$
intersections; $t \in \Tsp$ integer steps; $p \in \Psp_i$ phases
($p_b^i$ bus phase); $m \in \Msp_i$ movements; $v \in \Vsp$ classes;
$b \in \Bsp$ buses with route $\Rcal_b$; $s \in \Ssp$ sections;
$(i,j) \in \Esp$ links; $c$ cycle windows.  Every constraint below is
written in canonical form $a^\top z \le b$ or $a^\top z = b$: lower bounds
appear with negated coefficients.  No $\min$, $\max$, $\mathrm{abs}$,
$\bmod$, or ratio appears in any constraint; products of a binary and a
bounded variable are avoided by construction.

\paragraph{Signal-switching physics.}
\begin{align}
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} x_{i,p,t} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_excl} \\[3pt]
  G_i^{\min}\,(x_{i,p,t} - x_{i,p,t-1}) - \sum_{r=t}^{\min(t+G_i^{\min}-1,T)} x_{i,p,r} &\le 0
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_dwell} \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p,t} + x_{i,p',t+r} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; (p,p') \in \mathcal{F}_i,\; r=1,\dots,I_{\text{ig}},\; t \le T-r \label{eq:gate_clear}
\end{align}

\paragraph{Green and cycle bounds.}
\begin{align}
  -\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le -G_i^{\min}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; c \label{eq:g_bounds} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le G_i^{\max}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; c \\[3pt]
  -\sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le -(C_i^{\min}-L_i)
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c \label{eq:c_bounds} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le C_i^{\max}-L_i
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c
\end{align}

\paragraph{Flow and storage.}
\begin{align}
  Q_{i,m,v,t+1} - Q_{i,m,v,t} - A_{i,m,v,t} + d_{i,m,v,t} &= 0
    && \forall i,m,v,\; t < T \label{eq:mv_q} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} d_{i,m,v,t} - \mu_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le 0
    && \forall i,m,t \label{eq:mv_dcap} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,m,v,t} - Q_{i,m,v,t} - A_{i,m,v,t} &\le 0
    && \forall i,m,v,t \\[3pt]
  Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - Q_{i,m,v,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:mv_wait} \\[3pt]
  Q^w_{i,m,v,t} + K_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le K_{i,m} \\[3pt]
  Q_{i,m,v,t} - Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - K_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  \sum_{m \in \Msp_i(s,v)} Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - D_{i,s,v,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:mv_waitsum} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} Q_{i,m,v,t} - K_{i,m} &\le 0 \label{eq:storage_cap} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} d_{i,m,v,t} + \sum_{v \in \Vsp} Q_{j,m^+(m),v,t} - K_{j,m^+(m)} + M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - x_{i,p(m),t}) &\le M^{\text{big}}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; m,\; t \label{eq:discharge_space}
\end{align}
where $A_{i,m,v,t}$ is the realised arrival count (endogenous through
\cref{eq:cons_phase}) and $p(m)$ is the phase serving movement $m$.

\paragraph{Network conservation.}
\begin{align}
  N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t} - n_{ij,v,t} - \sum_{m \in \Msp_i:\, m \to (i,j)} d_{i,m,v,t-\tau_{ij}} &= 0
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; v,\; t \label{eq:cons_link} \\[3pt]
  A_{j,m,v,t} - \sum_{i:\,(i,j)\in\Esp} \gamma^m_{ij}\, N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t} - n^m_{j,v,t} &= 0
    && \forall j,m,v,t \label{eq:cons_phase}
\end{align}
where $\gamma^m_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$ is the fixed geometry mapping link $(i,j)$
to movement $m$ at $j$, and terms with $t-\tau_{ij}<0$ are zero.

\paragraph{Corridor coordination (lagged progression).}
\begin{align}
  h_{ij,t} - x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:band_min} \\[3pt]
  h_{ij,t} - x_{j,p_b^j,t+\ell_{ij}} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p_b^i,t} + x_{j,p_b^j,t+\ell_{ij}} - 1 - h_{ij,t} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  -\sum_{t \in \Tsp} h_{ij,t} &\le -B^{\min}_{ij}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp
\end{align}
with $x_{j,p,t+\ell} := 0$ when $t+\ell > T$.

\paragraph{Bus clocks and schedule adherence.}
\begin{align}
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} e_{b,i,t} - 1 &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_assign} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} t\, e_{b,i,t} - \tau_{b,i} &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_link} \\[3pt]
  e_{b,i,t} - x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\le 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog_bus} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i_h} + \tau^{\text{ff}}_{i_h,i_{h+1}} - \tau_{b,i_{h+1}} &\le 0
    && \forall b,\; h=0,\dots,N-1 \label{eq:arr_prop} \\[3pt]
  \tau_b^{\text{detect}} - \tau_{b,i_0} &\le 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp \label{eq:arr_init} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i} - \sigma^+_{b,i} + \sigma^-_{b,i} &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_split} \\[3pt]
  \sigma^+_{b,i} - s^3_{b,i} &\le \epsilon^{\text{late}}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_cap}
\end{align}

\paragraph{Domains.}
\begin{align*}
  & x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\},\quad h_{ij,t} \in \{0,1\},\quad e_{b,i,t} \in \{0,1\}, \\
  & Q_{i,m,v,t}, d_{i,m,v,t}, Q^w_{i,m,v,t}, D_{i,s,v,t}, s^3_{b,i} \in \Z_{\ge 0}, \\
  & \tau_{b,i}, \sigma^\pm_{b,i} \in \Z_{\ge 0}.
\end{align*}

\paragraph{Re-optimisation.} \label{sec:offset_recourse} Re-posing the same program from a measured
state $\hat{X}_{t_c}$ over a receding window $\Tsp(t_c)$ is the same
program with $X_{t_c}=\hat{X}_{t_c}$; the offset correction is the
difference between the new and old optimal offsets.  This is the exact
counterpart of the online recourse, and it is always feasible because the
all--no-action continuation is feasible.
\begin{equation}
  \text{\cref{eq:opt_crit} re-posed on } \Tsp(t_c) \text{ with }
  X_{t_c}=\hat{X}_{t_c}.
  \label{eq:reopt}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Reported (non-constraint) quantities.} Realised green is the open
indicator itself,
\begin{equation}
  g_{i,p,t} = x_{i,p,t} \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:gate_green}
\end{equation}
and the following are read off the committed schedule and are not variables of
the program:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Offset} $\phi_{i,c}$: the first step $t \in W_{i,c}$ with
        $x_{i,p_b^i,t}=1$.
  \item \textbf{Realised green} $g_{i,p,t} = x_{i,p,t}$, so $g=x$ (\cref{eq:gate_green}).
  \item \textbf{Green split} $G_{i,p,c} = \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t}$.
  \item \textbf{Cycle} $C_{i,c} = \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} G_{i,p,c} + L_i$.
  \item \textbf{Action label} at $(i,t)$: the letter ($\texttt{GE}$,
        $\texttt{INS}$, $\texttt{ER}$, $\texttt{GR}$, $\texttt{VP}$,
        $\texttt{PT}$, $\texttt{OC}$, $\texttt{NA}$) whose gate perturbation
        best matches the change in $x_{i,\cdot,t}$ relative to the warm start
        $\bar{x}_{i,\cdot,t}$; the online controller of
        \cref{sec:solutions} chooses the letter directly.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Relationship to the Online Controller}
\label{sec:milp_online_map}

The exact program decides the gate schedule; the online controller of
\cref{sec:solutions} decides \emph{action letters} that perturb a warm-start
schedule one event at a time.  The two are connected by the gate perturbation
each letter induces, which is the same mechanism the program prices through
the queue recursion:
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Letter & Gate perturbation (relative to warm start $\bar{x}$) & Cycle effect \\
\hline
\texttt{GE} & extends the bus-phase green by $\delta$ steps & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{INS} & inserts a $\delta$-step bus phase & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{ER} & truncates the bus-phase green by $\delta$ steps & $-\delta$ \\
\texttt{GR} & moves $\delta$ steps from a donor phase to the bus phase & $0$ \\
\texttt{VP} & skips intermediate phases (zero green on them) & $-\lvert\text{skipped}\rvert$ \\
\texttt{PT} & reorders the phase sequence & $0$ \\
\texttt{OC} & shifts the whole plan by $\Delta$ steps & $0$ net \\
\texttt{NA} & the warm start itself & $0$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Because the program's decision is the gate schedule, none of these letters is a
variable of \cref{sec:milp}; the online controller chooses a letter, applies
its gate perturbation to the warm start, and commits the resulting schedule
for the integer duration it selects.  This is the sense in which the action
space of \cref{sec:actions} is the \emph{candidate generator} for the
program's decision, and the program is the yardstick it is measured against.

\subsection{Model boundary}
\label{sec:milp_boundary}

The program is an \emph{exact} MILP of a \emph{simplified} plant: a
point-queue network with finite link storage, integer clocks, and a fixed
cycle envelope.  It is not an exact optimisation of the LWR kinematic-wave,
shockwave, or cell-transmission dynamics described in \cref{sec:dynamics};
those are background \emph{estimation} models used by the online layer
(\cref{sec:solutions}) to predict queues and ETAs, and they enter this
program only through the exogenous arrivals $n_{i,m,v,t}$ and the storage
bounds $K_{i,m}$.  Likewise, the program does not model moving bottlenecks
or spillback beyond the finite-storage block (\cref{eq:storage_cap,eq:discharge_space}).
Stating the boundary precisely is what makes the word ``exact'' honest: the
program is exact with respect to the plant it defines, and the plant is
deliberately simpler than the traffic it approximates.
