\subsection{Logan Road Corridor}
\label{sec:logan}

The second evaluation corridor is Logan Road (Brisbane, Australia), a longer and
more complex arterial than Kelvin Grove and a deliberately harder test for
corridor-level TSP. It is modelled in Aimsun Next
(\texttt{logan\_road\_new/}) as a \emph{single} arterial corridor:
The corridor configuration lists 23 signalised junctions in route
order (\cref{fig:logan}), \emph{all} TSP-controlled
(\texttt{TSP\_ACTIVE\_INTERSECTIONS} is \texttt{None}, so no junction is
excluded from control).  The two four-junction lists in
\texttt{INTERSECTION\_GROUPS} are the corridor coordinator's pre-arm groups,
not a control mask and not separate branches.  39 PT lines cross the corridor. Logan carries substantially heavier and more
variable cross-street demand, so the cross-traffic cost of any granted priority
is larger; in the shared code base it is the corridor on which un-coordinated
priority most readily gridlocks. No Logan results are reported in this paper:
the setup is a pre-registered evaluation protocol, and no conclusion drawn
anywhere in \cref{sec:results,sec:conclusions} rests on Logan.

