\subsection{Batch Experiment Design}
\label{sec:batch}

The evaluation is a three-phase scripted Aimsun Next batch.  Every strategy
is scored on the same objective, the served-passenger rate per delay-hour
(higher is better); the headline tables below report it equivalently as
total passenger delay (pax\,hrs, lower is better), which the objective
normalises.  Each phase asks one question, varies one thing, and fixes
everything else (\cref{tab:batch_design}).

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Batch experiment design: what was done in each phase.  Every cell is
a distinct random-seed replication of the full 1.25\,h corridor simulation at
the stated demand; learners pre-train on four held-out seeds (800--1100) and
are frozen before scoring, so no arm is scored on a seed it trained on.}
\label{tab:batch_design}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{2.6cm}p{3.4cm}p{5.2cm}r}
\hline
Phase (question) & What varies & What is fixed & Runs \\
\hline
1. Strategy comparison (which controller wins at nominal demand?) & 15 strategy arms $\times$ 5 eval seeds (300--700) $\times$ demand $\times$1.0 & Geometry, demand profile, measured state feed, guard protocol & 95 \\
2. Bus-demand sensitivity (does the winner survive bus-service change?) & Phase-1 champion + fixed-time baseline $\times$ 5 bus-service levels $\times$ 5 seeds & Strategy set, car demand, geometry & 50 \\
3. Demand and intervention sweep (does the winner survive demand change?) & Champion $\times$ 6 car-demand scalars (0.6--1.6) $\times$ 5 seeds $\times$ 3 action-family tactics (timing: GE$+$GR; swaps: INS$+$early-red; both) $\times$ 3 intervention spacings (default / 40\,s / 120\,s) + matched baseline & Geometry, bus service & 300 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Phase~1 -- strategy comparison (champion search).}
Fifteen strategy arms are evaluated over \emph{five} random-seed replications
(seeds 300, 400, 500, 600, 700) at demand scalar~1.0; each replication is the
full 1.25\,h corridor simulation. The five seeds are the apples-to-apples
scoring set for \emph{every} arm. The learning arms (the \texttt{CELLQLEARN}
family and \texttt{DCTSP\_MARL\_RL}) additionally pre-train on four held-out
seeds (800--1100) and are then frozen before scoring, so no arm is scored on a
seed it trained on. All arms decide on the same \emph{measured} state feed --
verified section-total flow and a measured queue floor -- rather than
an analytic warm-up estimate. The 15 arms are:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \texttt{NO\_TSP} -- baseline fixed-time control.
  \item \texttt{CELLQLEARN} -- CTM Q-learning (CellQLearn, \cref{sec:sol_cellqlearn}).
  \item \texttt{CELLQLEARN\_GATED} -- CellQLearn with the \emph{total-passenger
        net-benefit gate} and the \emph{projected-saturation downstream cascade
        cost} (\cref{sec:gate_cascade}); the corridor champion.
  \item \texttt{CELLQLEARN\_SAFE}, \texttt{CELLQLEARN\_FORCED} -- conservative
        and unguarded CellQLearn ablations.
  \item \texttt{DCTSP\_ZIG} -- Harmony Search + LWR gate (HSLWR / WaveGate).
  \item \texttt{DCTSP\_MP\_ECTM} -- CTM grid search (CTMGS / CellSearch).
  \item \texttt{DCTSP\_BARGAIN\_SPM}, \texttt{NASH\_BARGAIN} -- corridor Nash
        bargaining, weighted-sum and Nash-product variants (NashHS).
  \item \texttt{DCTSP\_MARL} (heuristic MARL) and \texttt{DCTSP\_MARL\_RL}
        (tabular CPD-QL learner).
  \item \texttt{CENTRALISED} -- per-second corridor controller
        (\cref{sec:sol_centralised}).
  \item \texttt{MAXPRESSURE\_FIX}, \texttt{MAXPRESSURE\_FLEX} -- passenger-weighted
        max-pressure with transit priority (\cref{sec:maxpressure}); and
        \texttt{MAXPRESSURE\_LB}, which adds the dwelling-bus lane-blockage and
        corridor-coordination terms of MP-TSP-LB \citep{Kobeissi2026MPTSPLB}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Phase~2 -- bus-demand sensitivity.}
The Phase-1 champion and the fixed-time baseline are swept over five
bus-service levels (timetable frequency multipliers 0.5--3.0) at fixed car
demand, five seeds each: this isolates whether the win depends on how many
buses are on the road.

\textbf{Phase~3 -- demand and intervention sweep.}
The champion is swept over the car-demand, tactic, and spacing factorial of
\cref{tab:batch_design} with a matched baseline at every demand level.
This isolates whether the corridor-champion's benefit holds away from the
nominal operating point.

All experiments share the same network geometry and demand profile; each cell is
a distinct random-seed replication.

