\textbf{Key findings (Kelvin Grove corridor, five-seed means -- statistical limits in Discussion):}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Only the gated CellQLearn improves on average.}
\texttt{CELLQLEARN\_GATED} cuts total passenger delay by $5.5\%$ (mean over five
seeds), with buses $8.4\%$ faster (15.2 vs.\ 16.6\,s/pax) and cars $4.8\%$
faster -- the only arm to improve both classes on average. The net-benefit gate
and downstream cascade cost (\cref{sec:gate_cascade}) are what convert
CellQLearn from a net-negative arm to a win: the un-gated \texttt{CELLQLEARN}
is $+4.7\%$ under the same learner, so the gate accounts for the entire swing.
\item \textbf{The strongest bus savings come at a car cost.} The MARL heuristic
(\texttt{DCTSP\_MARL}) gives the best bus delay ($13.3$\,s/pax, $-20\%$) and the
passenger-weighted max-pressure controllers give $-15\%$ bus delay, but both
raise car delay enough to net $-1.7\%$ (MARL) to $+8\%$ (max-pressure) total --
transit priority that helps buses while modestly loading the cross street.
\item \textbf{A cluster of methods gridlocks catastrophically.} The Centralised
per-second controller ($+58\%$), Nash-product bargaining ($+55\%$), CTM grid
search ($+49\%$), and weighted Nash bargaining ($+31\%$) all explode total delay,
driven entirely by cross-street car delay (27--34\,s/pax vs.\ 18.9 baseline).
These arms do not act \emph{more} than the champion (\cref{tab:kg_tsp});
they act where the released platoon gridlocks a downstream link the single-cell
model cannot see.
\item \textbf{Un-coordinated per-junction priority is a seed lottery.} With a
local cost model, the same action rate is beneficial on some demand
realisations and gridlocking on others; the per-action predicted benefit is
essentially uncorrelated with the realised outcome. The net-benefit gate plus
projected-saturation cascade is the mechanism that prices the network effect and
removes the catastrophic tail -- \texttt{CELLQLEARN\_GATED} has the tightest
five-seed spread of any acting arm.
\item \textbf{Offset recoverability separates a helpful corridor from a
harmful one.} A cycle-lengthening grant only preserves the coordinated offset
if the seconds it borrows can be trimmed back the same cycle
(\cref{eq:online_recover}, headroom $\hat{R}_{i,t}$ of
\cref{eq:recoverable}). On Kelvin Grove the cross phases carry slack, so
extensions recover and TSP nets a gain; on the saturated Logan Road corridor the
cross phases sit at their minimum green ($\hat{R}_{i,t}\!\to\!0$), so an ungated
extension cannot be repaid, the offset drifts, and the green wave breaks -- the
lose--lose outcome where both bus \emph{and} car delay rise. Enforcing the
recoverability constraint (clamp to $\hat{R}_{i,t}$, else stand down; prefer the
cycle-neutral GR/OC actions) makes the acting arms hold the coordinated plan
rather than degrade it, which is why the champion's correct behaviour on Logan is
to stand down to $\approx$NO\_TSP rather than to intervene.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Demand sensitivity (Phase 3).}  Sweeping the corridor champion across six
car-demand scalars (0.6--1.6) shows the benefit is concentrated near the nominal
operating point: at scalar~1.0 the champion reduces delay, but at both lower and
higher demand its most-frequent-intervention setting is roughly neutral and its
default-frequency setting is mildly harmful. The corridor-level gain therefore
does \emph{not} robustly generalise across the full demand range -- a limitation
we attribute to the residual mismatch between the priced (local\,+\,one-route
cascade) cost and the true network response under heavy loading.

