\subsection{Runtime admissibility rules: which actions the live controller is allowed to take each tick}
\label{sec:online_guards}

By a \emph{guard} we mean a runtime admissibility rule: a check the live controller
applies at every decision tick that must pass before a proposed action may be
committed. The set of these rules is the online counterpart of the exact
program's hard constraints --- the exact program enforces them over a clairvoyant
horizon, whereas online they are evaluated on the estimated state, one tick at a
time. Concretely they answer ``is this action allowed \emph{right now}?'' (e.g.\
is a bus in the detection window, has the cooldown elapsed, would the extension
break recoverability or the green band); a candidate is committed only if it
clears all of them. We write the admissible set as $\hat{\Ucal}(\hat{X}_t)$.

Let $\hat{X}_t$ denote the estimated state at epoch $t \in \Tsp^{\text{trig}}$: live phase and
remaining time, measured approach/cross queues $\hat{Q}_{i,p,t}$ for every
$i$ (including each downstream integer $j$ with $(i,j)\in\Esp$), bus ETA
$\hat{\eta}$ with uncertainty $\hat{\sigma}$ (absent when no bus is present),
schedule deviation
$\hat{\sigma}^+$, downstream saturation derived from $\hat{Q}_{j,t}$, live
POG $\hat{\pi}^{\text{POG}}_{i,t} \in [0,1]$, phase pressures, and
grant history.  A candidate $(\hat{k},\hat{\delta})$ with
integer $\hat{\delta}$ is admissible only in
$\hat{\Ucal}(\hat{X}_t)$:

\paragraph{Online causality axioms (declared).} Two ordering facts the exact
program needs no constraint for (it is joint over the horizon) but the
online layer must \emph{state} explicitly, because they are what make an
action taken at one junction propagate to the decisions taken downstream:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Causal commit order.} Within an epoch $t$, junctions are
        solved in corridor order, upstream to downstream along each route
        group.  The estimate $\hat{X}_{j,t}$ on which downstream junction $j$
        decides is the state \emph{after} the upstream commits of the same
        epoch have been applied -- live phase, durations, and offsets already
        updated -- so a grant at $i$ is visible to $j$'s decision the same
        step, never guessed from pre-commit state.  Formally, the online
        solver processes $i \prec j$ when $i$ is upstream of $j$ on the
        corridor (the travel lag $\ell_{ij}$ orders them), and
        $j$'s admissible set is evaluated on
        $\hat{X}_{j,t}(\text{after } i\text{'s commit})$:
        \begin{equation}
          \hat{y}_{j,k,t} \;\in\; \hat{\Ucal}\bigl(\hat{X}_{j,t} \circ
            \{u_{i,t} : i \prec j\}\bigr)
          \label{eq:online_causal}
        \end{equation}
        where $\circ$ composes the committed upstream actions into the state.
  \item \textbf{Plan-visible state.} The estimate $\hat{X}_t$ carries, for
        every corridor neighbour, its \emph{last-committed} plan (durations,
        bus-green length, cycle, offset) -- not the baseline -- so the delay,
        ETA, recoverable-headroom, and progression estimates any junction
        makes price the real corridor timing, and a downstream pre-arm is
        re-evaluated against a changed upstream plan rather than fired on a
        stale window.  This is the online twin of the lagged mismatch
        (the travel lag) and the gate schedule
        (the gate schedule): the push of the chosen plan to the state
        is what closes the loop.
\end{enumerate}
With these declared, ``an action taken changes the dynamics downstream'' is
not an emergent accident but a stated invariant of the online layer: the
solver always acts on a state that already contains the corridor's latest
committed timing.
\begin{align}
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} \hat{y}_{i,k,t} &\le 1 - \hat{z}_{i,t}^{\text{far}}
    && \text{(detection window; $=1$ on bus-free timer/empty epochs)} \label{eq:online_det} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{\tau=t}^{t+\kappa_{\text{cool}}-1}\sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} \hat{y}_{i,k,\tau} &\le 1
    && \text{(cooldown / min spacing)} \label{eq:online_cool} \\[3pt]
  \hat{y}_{i,\text{GE},t} + \hat{y}_{i,\text{PI},t} &\le 1 - \hat{\nu}_{i,t}^{\text{spill}}
    && \text{(spillback block)} \label{eq:online_spill} \\[3pt]
  \hat{y}_{i,\text{GE},t} \le \hat{\alpha}_{i,t},\quad
  \hat{y}_{i,\text{PI},t} &\le 1 - \hat{\alpha}_{i,t}
    && \text{(phase validity: GE iff bus-green, PI iff red)} \label{eq:online_phase} \\[3pt]
  \hat{\delta}\;\mathbf{1}\!\left[\hat{k}\in\Ksp^{\text{len}}\right]
    &\le \hat{R}_{i,t}
    && \text{(recoverability / offset preservation)} \label{eq:online_recover} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} \hat{y}_{i,k,t} &\le 1 - \mathbf{1}[t - \hat{t}^{\text{phase}}_{i} < T^{\text{minActive}}]
    && \text{(min phase-active dwell)} \label{eq:online_minactive} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} \hat{y}_{i,k,t} &\le 1 - \hat{h}_{i,t}
    && \text{(NA hold $H^{\text{hold}}$)} \label{eq:online_hold} \\[3pt]
  \hat{L}^{\text{prog}}_{i,t}(\hat{\delta}) &\le P^{\text{max}} + M^{\text{big}}\Bigl(1 - \sum_{k \in \Ksp^{\text{len}}} \hat{y}_{i,k,t}\Bigr)
    && \text{(Purdue POG gate)} \label{eq:online_purdue} \\[3pt]
  \hat{\Psi}_{i,t} - \hat{\Lambda}_{i,t} &\ge G^{\text{mon}}_{\min} - M^{\text{big}}\,\hat{b}^{\text{present}}_{i,t} - M^{\text{big}}\Bigl(1 - \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} \hat{y}_{i,k,t}\Bigr)
    && \text{(bus-free monitor warrant)} \label{eq:online_monitor}
\end{align}
The spillback flag $\hat{\nu}_{i,t}^{\text{spill}}$ that \cref{eq:online_spill}
blocks on is defined here, with the rest of the estimated-state logic, rather
than among the program's own constraints:
\begin{equation}
  \hat{\nu}_{i,t}^{\text{spill}} = 1 \iff \hat{Q}_{j,t} \ge Q^{\text{jam}}_{j}
  \;\; \text{for some $(i,j) \in \Esp$},
  \label{eq:online_spill_def}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{z}^{\text{far}} = 1$ beyond the ETA-reliability horizon (and $0$ on
bus-free timer/empty epochs, so \cref{eq:online_det} never blocks the monitor),
$\kappa_{\text{cool}}$ is the integer cooldown (one grant per cycle plus
$\max(30, 0.3\,C_i)$ spacing in the runs),
$\hat{\nu}_{i,t}^{\text{spill}}$ is the measured-state spillback flag of
\cref{eq:online_spill_def} ($\hat{Q}_{j,t}=\sum_p\hat{Q}_{j,p,t}$ at
downstream integer $j$, same $t$, thresholded at $Q^{\text{jam}}_{j}$),
$\hat{\alpha}_{i,t} = 1$ iff the bus phase is live (wrong-phase GE is
rejected here), $\hat{t}^{\text{phase}}_{i}$ is the start step of the current
phase (so $t - \hat{t}^{\text{phase}}_{i}$ is the phase-active dwell checked by
\cref{eq:online_minactive} against $T^{\text{minActive}}$),
$\hat{h}_{i,t} = 1$ inside the $H^{\text{hold}}$-step NA hold of
\cref{eq:online_hold} (\texttt{NO\_ACTION\_HOLD\_S}, default 3\,s),
$\hat{L}^{\text{prog}}_{i,t}(\hat{\delta})$ is the estimated displaced-platoon
loss $\hat{\pi}^{\text{POG}}_{i,t}\,\hat{q}^{\text{main}}_{i,t}\,\hat{\delta}^2\,\rho_{\text{car}}$
(code \texttt{\_progression\_cost}: live POG $\times$ main flow $\times$
shift-squared $\times$ occupancy, exact counterpart
\cref{eq:pog_ewma,eq:pog_loss_zero}), $\hat{b}^{\text{present}}_{i,t} = 1$
iff a bus supports the epoch, and $\hat{\Psi}_{i,t}/\hat{\Lambda}_{i,t}$ are the
estimated bus saving/car cost in pax\,s.  The last line, \cref{eq:online_recover}, is the
\emph{recoverability} (offset-preservation) guard added here as an explicit
decision constraint.  A cycle-\emph{lengthening} letter
$\Ksp^{\text{len}}=\{\text{GE},\text{PI},\text{VP},\text{PT}\}$ borrows
$\hat{\delta}$ seconds that must be trimmed back from the upcoming cross phases
\emph{within the same cycle} so the junction stays locked to its coordinated
progression overlap (\cref{eq:band_min}); the recoverable headroom is
\begin{equation}
  \hat{R}_{i,t} \;=\; \sum_{p \in \hat{\Psp}_{i,t}^{\uparrow}}
    \min\!\Bigl(\hat{g}_{i,p,t} - G_i^{\min},\; \tfrac{1}{2}\,\hat{g}_{i,p,t}\Bigr)^{+},
  \label{eq:recoverable}
\end{equation}
the slack of the phases still to run this cycle
($\hat{\Psp}_{i,t}^{\uparrow}$, excluding the bus phase) above their minimum
green $G_i^{\min}$, capped at half of each phase.  A grant with
$\hat{\delta} > \hat{R}_{i,t}$ cannot be repaid this cycle, so the offset
\emph{drifts permanently} and the green wave breaks at the downstream integer
$j$--the coordination cascade.  The controller therefore clamps
$\hat{\delta}\!\leftarrow\!\hat{R}_{i,t}$, or stands down when
$\hat{R}_{i,t}<G^{\min}_{\text{act}}$.  GR (green reallocation) and OC (offset
correction) are cycle-\emph{neutral}--they redistribute green within a fixed
cycle length--so they carry no debt and are exempt, which makes them the
structure-preserving fallback when \cref{eq:online_recover} binds.

\paragraph{Purdue (POG) gate, timing gates, and bus-free operation (what the new lines do).}
\cref{eq:online_purdue} is the online Purdue Coordination Diagram veto
(\texttt{PROGRESSION\_GATE}): the controller samples $\hat{\pi}^{\text{POG}}_{i,t}$
(\texttt{\_sample\_progression}, EWMA weight $0.08$) and refuses an
offset-shifting grant whose displaced main-platoon pax\,s exceeds
$P^{\text{max}} = 300$\,pax\,s (\texttt{PROGRESSION\_MAX\_LOSS\_PAXS})--it
refuses to push its own coordinated platoon off green.  The learner variant
additionally pays $w^{\text{POG}}\hat{L}^{\text{prog}}$ inside its reward
(\texttt{BXT\_POG\_REWARD}/\texttt{BXT\_POG\_WEIGHT}), so coordination is both
vetoed and taught.  \cref{eq:online_minactive,eq:online_hold} are the
controller-timing rules (\texttt{MIN\_PHASE\_ACTIVE\_S},
\texttt{NO\_ACTION\_HOLD\_S}): a just-started phase runs undisturbed, and a NA
outcome holds before re-checking (a phase boundary re-opens evaluation
regardless).  Rule~C truncation (\texttt{EMPTY\_PHASE\_SKIP\_MODE}: current
pressure $\le Q^{\text{empty}} = 1$\,pax with best-other pressure $\ge
Q^{\text{press}} = 5$\,pax, min-green floored) advances an empty phase toward
pressure with no bus required.  \cref{eq:online_monitor} is the bus-free
warrant: under \texttt{CONTINUOUS\_MONITOR\_MODE} the controller evaluates the
action set on the current traffic state every $T^{\text{mon}}$ even with
$\hat{b}^{\text{present}}_{i,t} = 0$ (car-only objective, bus term $0$), and
commits only when the net car-plus-Purdue gain clears $G^{\text{mon}}_{\min} =
50$\,pax\,s; a bus, when present, restores the passenger-weighted bus term and
R1 governs.  Together these four lines are exactly when the controller may
apply an action--not just which action is cheapest--and they operate with or
without buses, which is why the exact program of Sec.~\ref{sec:bus_optional}
is stated trigger-agnostically.

\paragraph{Recovered-cycle plan (bounded multi-cycle transition).}
When a single cycle cannot absorb the debt but the grant is still worth serving,
the controller admits any $\hat{\delta}$ recoverable within a bounded horizon and
repays it over several cycles---the classical signal-\emph{transition}/recovery
plan. With per-cycle recoverable slack $\hat{R}_{i,t}$, the offset re-locks in
\begin{equation}
  K^{\star}_{i,t} \;=\; \Bigl\lceil\, \hat{\delta} \,/\, \hat{R}_{i,t} \,\Bigr\rceil
  \;\le\; K_{\max}
  \label{eq:recover_cycles}
\end{equation}
cycles, trimming at most $\hat{R}_{i,t}$ each cycle---distributed proportional to
each phase's slack so the fractional trim is equalised and no phase is starved,
and bounded by the transition rate $\tau$ inside $\hat{R}_{i,t}$ so the
per-cycle correction stays smooth. $K_{\max}=1$ (default) is exactly the strict
same-cycle constraint \cref{eq:online_recover}; $K_{\max}>1$ trades a longer
but \emph{bounded} transition for the ability to serve a genuinely delayed bus on
a corridor with little per-cycle slack, without the permanent drift an
un-recovered grant would cause (\cref{eq:online_recover} with the
$K_{\max}\hat{R}_{i,t}$ budget admits it; the plan of \cref{eq:recover_cycles}
returns it to coordination). Cycle-neutral GR/OC need $K^{\star}=0$.

This
guard is decisive for the corridor difference in
\cref{sec:results}: on Kelvin Grove the cross phases carry slack
($\hat{R}_{i,t}$ large) so extensions recover and TSP helps, whereas on the
saturated Logan Road corridor the cross phases sit near $G_i^{\min}$
($\hat{R}_{i,t}\!\to\!0$), so an ungated extension cannot recover and drives the
lose--lose cascade; enforcing \cref{eq:online_recover} makes the acting arms
stand down to the coordinated plan instead of degrading it.  Every guard above
is therefore a function of
$\hat{X}_t$ at the same $(i,t)$--or, for spillback and recoverability, of
downstream/upcoming state within $\hat{X}_t$--so the online program is closed
exactly as the exact program is: upstream allowance reads downstream queue
state.
On top of feasibility, the scored runs apply: a
congestion/viability gate (stand down when cross-pax/bus-pax $>$ 2.5 or
saturation $>$ 0.65--0.75), a cost veto
($\text{car-cost} > \text{ratio} \times \text{bus-benefit}$, ratio 0.8--3.0
per arm), a minimum-gain warrant ($\hat{\delta}$ must save 2--15\,s per arm),
and, on gated arms, the total-passenger net-benefit gate plus
projected-saturation cascade (Sec.~\ref{sec:gate_cascade}).  A takeover
promotion ($\hat{\sigma}^+ > \sigma^{\text{takeover}}$) relaxes the detection
and cooldown lines for critically late buses.  Variable-phase skipping and
phase rotation (Secs.~\ref{sec:phase_skip}--\ref{sec:phase_rot}, with
invalid-action masking and Webster re-splits) are candidate generators
subject to this same $\hat{\Ucal}$; they are not exact-plant constraints.

Each method's role is to \emph{propose and evaluate} candidate actions with
its own search strategy and traffic-state model subject to
$\hat{\Ucal}(\hat{X}_t)$; the final commit decision is
the scalar $\arg\min\Delta_{\text{net}}$ defined below.  Candidate
generators may use weighted internal scores to rank proposals (see the
``Actual reward'' column of \cref{tab:methods}); the commit step never
does--methods therefore differ in \emph{which} candidates they generate and
\emph{how accurately} they estimate each candidate's scalar net cost, never
in how trade-offs are weighted at commit.  Executable step-by-step versions of every family below are given
as pseudocode in \cref{sec:appendix_pseudo} (Algorithms~A0--A8):
the appendix is the unambiguous reference for what each controller does at
each event, while this section explains why.

\subsection{Layered online control: fixed-time base, an actuated layer, and the algorithm overlay}
\label{sec:layered_control}

The deployed signals run \emph{fixed-time} coordinated plans (long main-street
greens with short side phases; the network export carries only per-phase
durations, no gap/max/force-off), so a well-timed plan already organises
platoons into a green band and is a strong baseline. The controllers add two
online layers on top of that fixed base, both operating on the estimated state
$\hat{X}_t$ and always subject to the admissibility set $\hat{\Ucal}(\hat{X}_t)$
of Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}. The fixed base plan itself is not solved here: it
is \emph{given} --- the hand-tuned coordinated timing from the field, with common
cycle $C$, green splits $g_{i,p}$ ($p\in\Psp_i$) and offsets $\phi_i$ taken as
design data. Solving the base plan (Webster splits, green-wave offsets) is a
separate offline problem and deliberately out of scope: every result in this paper
measures what the online layers add on top of the plan the street already runs.
The published bus timetable is likewise given, not designed: line $\ell$ runs at
headway $h_\ell$, and each scheduled trip $b$ has a scheduled arrival at every
junction on its route $\Rcal_b$,
\begin{equation}
  \bar{\tau}_{b,i} \;=\; \tau^{\text{dep}}_b + \sum_{(k,k')\,\preceq\, i}
      \big(t^{\text{run}}_{kk'} + t^{\text{dwell}}_{k'}\big),
  \qquad b\in\Bsp,\; i\in\Rcal_b,
  \label{eq:timetable}
\end{equation}
so the timetable $\mathcal{T}=\{\bar{\tau}_{b,i}\}$ is known design data (departure
$\tau^{\text{dep}}_b$, scheduled run times $t^{\text{run}}$, dwells
$t^{\text{dwell}}$) --- the same $\bar{\tau}_{b,i}$ the online schedule cap
(\cref{eq:sched_cap}) already uses. No optimisation is performed over
$(C,g,\phi)$: there is no Webster program, no saturation cap, and no offset or
timetable band solved here. Whatever alignment the given plan has with the
timetable is inherited, not designed, and the online layers below are evaluated
against the plan as found --- including its mistimings, which are part of what
the methods must overcome rather than a baseline to be re-solved away.

\paragraph{No two-stage program.}
Because the plan is given, there is no here-and-now/recourse split and no
two-stage stochastic program: no expected-value plan, no wait-and-see bound,
and no \texttt{PLAN\_OPTIMIZE}/\texttt{PLAN\_OPT\_ONLY}/\texttt{PLAN\_OPT\_NASH}
variants. Demand $q_{i,p}(\omega)$ varies and each bus arrives off schedule,
$\tau_{b,i}(\omega)=\bar{\tau}_{b,i}+\xi_{b,i}(\omega)$, and the online
controller is evaluated directly on the realised scenarios against the fixed
plan --- the ``do nothing'' baseline is the given timing itself, and every
result below measures what the online layers add on top of it.

\paragraph{Layer 1 --- coordinated-actuated base (\texttt{ACTUATED\_BASE\_MODE}).}
Because a fixed plan is not demand-responsive, an optional base layer makes it so
without disturbing coordination. The \emph{coordinated} phase is identified as the
longest programmed phase (the band). At each epoch a non-coordinated phase is
\emph{gapped out} to min-green when its own served-approach measured queue falls
to $\le\epsilon^{\text{gap}}$, and \emph{maxed out} at $g^{\max}_{\text{act}}$;
the coordinated phase itself is never truncated. To keep the offset, the reclaimed
seconds are handed to the coordinated phase so the cycle length is held
(coordinated-actuated ``rest in green''). Since that retiming could itself drift
the offset, a self-verifying guard measures the realised cycle length each time
the coordinated phase starts and auto-disables the reclaim after $K^{\text{drift}}$
cycles that deviate from nominal by more than $\delta^{\text{drift}}$. This layer
is the online analogue of gap-out/max-out/force-off actuated control; the exact
program of Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards} treats it as part of the plant, not a
decision variable.

\paragraph{Layer 2 --- algorithm overlay, bus-triggered and continuous.} On top of
the base, each method (Nash bargaining, the CTM Q-learner, max-pressure, Harmony
Search, the centralised controller) proposes TSP actions. Two extensions widen
this overlay from bus-triggered to continuous. First, when no bus is present the
same bargaining game may still be played on the traffic state
(\texttt{NASH\_CONTINUOUS\_MODE}, \texttt{BXT\_CONTINUOUS\_MODE}): the transit term
is zero, so the payoff is the through-traffic served by extending the current
green, weighed against the cross cost, with bargaining power $p_T^{\text{cont}}$
(default $0$, i.e.\ minimise total passenger delay). Second, a bus-free corridor
coupling ($w^{\text{corr}}$) discounts each no-bus action by the downstream
landing cost of the green it adds, projected onto the next junctions' live signal
windows using corridor geometry rather than a tracked bus --- so a continuous
action that would push a platoon into a downstream red is penalised. When
\texttt{NASH\_MEASURED\_STATE} is set, the mainline benefit is bounded by the
\emph{measured} main-approach queue (not a saturation assumption) and the cross
cost is priced from the measured side feed, so the game reflects the realised
network state.

\paragraph{Purdue coordination in the learner's reward.} The learner's reward
additionally charges each offset-shifting action the displaced-platoon
passenger-seconds it causes ($w^{\text{POG}}$; Sec.~\ref{sec:objectives}), so the
learner is trained to prefer coordination-preserving actions rather than merely
being vetoed after the fact by the progression guard. All of the above are online
overlays: they change which candidates are generated and how each is scored, never
how the commit trade-off is weighted (Sec.~\ref{sec:selection_layer}).

\subsection{Commit Rules: the Z1 Selection Layer Is One of Five, Not a Universal Selector}
\label{sec:selection_layer}

The exact program commits its whole schedule at once against four measures
with distinct roles (Sec.~\ref{sec:objectives}): $Z_1$ is
minimised, $Z_3$ is capped (fallback penalty if infeasible), $Z_4$ is capped,
$Z_2$ is monitored only.  The online code does \emph{not} route every family
through a single $Z_1$ selector--the earlier draft claimed it did, which is
why the layer read as ``only $Z_1$''.  What the code actually does
(\texttt{MODE\_DECISION\_IS\_FINAL}: mode-commits-own-action) is: each family \emph{proposes} candidates with its own
ranking score, then \emph{commits} with its own decision rule.  Only the
delay-based families commit through the weight-free $Z_1$ layer below; the
others commit on fairness (Nash), stability (max-pressure), learned policy
(Q-argmax), or rules (centralised).  $Z_4$ is the one veto shared by (almost)
all commits; $Z_2$ never decides directly but enters through the
Purdue/cascade approximations; $Z_3$ enters through headway rewards,
takeover, and minimum-gain warrants.

\paragraph{The $Z_1$ commit (Type~A): the program's objective applied to one action.}
For the delay-based families (HSLWR, CTMGS, CellQLearn variants, MARL heuristic)
the commit applies the program's one objective to a single candidate:
$\Delta_{\text{net}}$ is the change in $Z_1$ over a local look-ahead window, in
the same passenger-second currency as \cref{eq:Z1}---car delay added, bus
passenger-delay saved:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:net_pick}
  \Delta_{\text{net}}(a) = \Delta_{\text{car}}^{\text{main}}(a)
    + \Delta_{\text{car}}^{\text{side}}(a)
    - \bigl(d_b^{\text{NA}} - d_b^{a}\bigr)\,n_b\,\rho_{\text{bus}}.
\end{equation}
Candidates are first put through the $Z_4$ gate
($Z_4(a) \le Z_4^{\text{fix}} + \varepsilon_{Z_4}$, \cref{eq:opt_crit}) and a
non-dominance filter (bookkeeping only---the scalar minimiser is always
non-dominated); among the survivors the committed action is
$\arg\min_{a}\Delta_{\text{net}}(a)$, with \texttt{NO\_ACTION} netting exactly
zero.  The rule is weight-free and is the program's $Z_1$ decision applied to one
action, not a new objective; it lives with the solution methods, not inside the
program, and binds only Type~A commits.

\paragraph{The other four commits (why $Z_1$ alone cannot explain the paper).}
