\subsection{Method I: Tabular Q-Learning (CPD-QL)}
\label{sec:sol_cpd}
% ----------------------------------------------------

CPD-QL learns action values $Q(s,a)$ for the single-event subproblem from
repeated experience.  The state $s$ is a discrete bin vector: headway deviation
(late/on-time/early), bus ETA to stop-line ($<$10 / [10,30) / $\ge$30\,s),
estimated delay (zero/low/high), and phase match (Boolean)--yielding
$3\times3\times3\times2 = 54$ states.

\noindent\textbf{Solves:} the single-event Q-learning problem: learn action
values $Q(s,a)$ maximising the headway-plus-delay reward below, then act
$\varepsilon$-greedily.  Nothing is optimised online beyond the argmax.

\paragraph{Objective mapping (Type~E, not Type~A).} CPD-QL proposes and commits
by $\arg\max_a Q(s,a)$ (Alg.~\ref{alg:cpdql}) with only the cost veto
$\theta{=}3.0$ and no $Z_1$ layer; its reward
Eq.~\eqref{eq:cpd_reward} is a genuine $Z_1{+}Z_3$ blend, so lateness is
optimised jointly with delay (Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives}, learned-commit column).

For action $a$, the reward is:
\begin{equation}
  r(a) = w_h\,\rho_{\text{bus}}\bigl(|\sigma_{\text{in}}| - |\sigma_{\text{out}}(a)|\bigr)
       + (1-w_h)\bigl((d_b^{\text{NA}} - d_b^{a})\,n_b - \Delta_{\text{car}}(a)\bigr)
  \label{eq:cpd_reward}
\end{equation}
with $w_h = 0.5$ (balanced headway/delay weighting).  Both terms are now in
the same currency, pax\,s: the headway term carries bus occupancy
$\rho_{\text{bus}}$ (matching the online Act step in
Alg.~\ref{alg:cpdql} and the deployed reward, where
$r = w_h\rho_{\text{bus}}(|\sigma_{\text{in}}|-|\sigma_{\text{out}}|) +
(1-w_h)(\text{bps}-\text{cpc})$), and the delay term carries the bus load
$n_b$ on the saved seconds minus the car passenger-seconds
$\Delta_{\text{car}}$.  A mixed-seconds reward would let $w_h$ blend
incommensurate units; the occupancy-scaled form keeps the objective a true
$Z_1{+}Z_3$ blend.
The Q-table is initialised from estimated immediate rewards
and updated via standard Q-learning with $\alpha_Q=0.1$, $\gamma_Q=0.9$,
$\varepsilon=0.1$.  Action selection is $\varepsilon$-greedy over a
7-action set $\{\text{NA}, \text{GE}_{\delta}: \delta \in
\{5,10,15\}\} \cup \{\text{INS}_{\delta}: \delta \in \{10,15,20\}\}$ --
an integer-second candidate set, deliberately sparse here (Method I is the
low-resolution baseline); the lattice-solved methods (deficit/DE/exact)
size the same tokens at any integer in the admissible band.

\noindent\textbf{Limitation:} CPD-QL has no hard pre-commit veto.  The
$\Delta_{\text{car}}$ term in Eq.\,\ref{eq:cpd_reward} penalises
net-negative actions in the reward, so the agent \emph{learns} to avoid
them, but nothing \emph{blocks} a harmful commit: under $\varepsilon$
exploration, and whenever the Q-values are still wrong early in training,
the $\arg\max$ can select an action whose true passenger cost exceeds its
bus benefit.  Other traffic is still priced in the reward, but only
softly, through learning rather than a gate.  This manifests as the
$+2.3\%$ total-delay increase scored in \cref{sec:res_overall}.

% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Method II: Harmony Search with LWR, (HSLWR)}
\label{sec:sol_wavegate}
% ----------------------------------------------------

WaveGate learns single-event durations with Harmony Search (HS)--a population-based
metaheuristic over a harmony memory of the $H$ best $(s,\delta)$ pairs--seeded by
a closed-form LWR shockwave duration and gated by a passenger cost-benefit test.
The full improvise--evaluate--update loop is stated executable-form in
Alg.~\ref{alg:wavegate}; what matters for the paper is the LWR seed and gate.

\noindent\textbf{Solves:} the integer duration $\delta$ minimising predicted
net passenger cost, searched by Harmony Search from the closed-form LWR seed
and admitted only through the balance rule \cref{eq:rule_balance} below.

\paragraph{Objective mapping (Type~A).} WaveGate proposes by Harmony Search on
the LWR cost $J(\delta)$ and commits through the $Z_1$ layer: grant iff
\cref{eq:rule_balance} holds, then $\arg\min\Delta_{\text{net}}$ s.t.\
the $Z_4$ veto (Alg.~\ref{alg:wavegate} via Alg.~A0); the cleanest Type~A
example (Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives}, $Z_1$-net column).

\paragraph{LWR shockwave gate.}
WaveGate is a shockwave-informed, benefit-gated optimisation method that grants transit signal priority only when LWR-estimated queue clearance yields a net passenger benefit.
The bus approach queue is modelled via the LWR back-shockwave:
\begin{equation}
  w = -\frac{q_{\text{arr}}}{k_j - k_{\text{arr}}},
  \quad
  \delta^* = \max\!\left(0,\; \frac{n_0}{q_{\text{sat}} - q_{\text{arr}}} - d_b^{\text{NA}}\right)
  \label{eq:lwr}
\end{equation}
clamped to $[\delta_{\min},\delta_{\max}]$.  Read it as a deficit: the queue
of $n_0$ vehicles ahead of the bus needs $n_0/(q_{\text{sat}} - q_{\text{arr}})$
seconds to discharge at net saturation rate (both in veh and veh/s, so the
ratio is seconds), while the bus already absorbs $d_b^{\text{NA}}$ seconds of
that wait under no action; the extension covers the remainder, floored at
zero.  The cost-benefit gate is the estimated-state form of the balance rule
\cref{eq:rule_balance}, evaluated per intersection $i$ on main section against
side section: TSP is granted at $i$ only when the main-section bus
passenger-seconds saved exceed the added side-section car passenger-seconds,
with $\rho_{\text{bus}}=40$ and $\rho_{\text{car}}=1.2$ folded into the delay
terms so both sides are in pax\,s.
$\Delta^{\text{main}}_{\text{car},i}$ is the car passenger-second change on
the bus's own (main) approach, negative when the platoon benefits, and
$\Delta^{\text{side}}_{\text{car},i}$ the added car passenger-seconds on
the held cross (side) approach.

The gate compares the main-section net passenger effect against the
side-section cost at the acting intersection and grants TSP only when the
net exceeds the cost by the calibrated warrant.  Other traffic is therefore
priced explicitly on both approaches, not ignored.  This prevents the
net-harmful over-triggering observed in
CPD-QL (\cref{sec:res_overall}).

\subsubsection{Shockwave Profile Reconstruction from Simulation Data}
\label{sec:shockwave_profile}

The LWR shockwave gate (Eq.\,\ref{eq:lwr}) requires four shockwave speeds
derived from the flow--density fundamental diagram.  Following
\citet{AnsariEsfeh2025TSPCV}
(Fig.\,3 therein), we define the four shockwave speeds at intersection $i$:
\begin{align}
  v_{q,i} &= -\frac{q_{\mathrm{in},i}}{k_{j,i} - k_{f,i}}
    \label{eq:vq} \\[4pt]
  v_{c,i} &= \phantom{-}\frac{q_{s,i}}{k_{j,i} - k_{c,i}}
    \label{eq:vc} \\[4pt]
  v_{p,i} &= \phantom{-}\frac{q_{s,i} - q_{\mathrm{in},i}}{k_{c,i} - k_{f,i}}
    \label{eq:vp} \\[4pt]
  v_{d,i} &= v_{f,i}
    \label{eq:vd}
\end{align}
where $q_{\mathrm{in},i}$ is the measured approach flow (veh/s),
$k_{j,i}$ is jam density (veh/m), $k_{f,i} = q_{\mathrm{in},i}/v_{f,i}$
is the free-flow density at input flow, $k_{c,i} = q_{s,i}/v_{f,i}$ is
capacity density, $q_{s,i}$ is saturation flow, and $v_{f,i}$ is free-flow
speed.  Physically:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item $v_{q,i} < 0$: backward-forming shock at the upstream edge of the
        stopped queue (queuing shockwave, negative = propagates upstream);
  \item $v_{c,i} > 0$: forward-moving shock as the queue discharges during
        green (discharging shockwave);
  \item $v_{p,i}$: speed of the density transition between the dense queue
        and the arriving free-flow stream (pressure shockwave);
  \item $v_{d,i} = v_{f,i}$: free-flow speed at which vehicles depart
        once the queue has cleared (departing shockwave).
\end{itemize}



% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Method III: Exhaustive Grid Search (CTMGS)}
\label{sec:sol_cellsearch}
% ----------------------------------------------------

\noindent\textbf{CTM and cascade.} The cell transition, projected saturation,
and downstream cascade cost are fixed once in Sec.~\ref{sec:ctm}
(Eqs.~\eqref{eq:ctm_send}--\eqref{eq:ctm_cascade}); CellSearch is their
brute-force user.

CellSearch exhaustively evaluates the integer grid
$\mathcal{G} = \{\delta_{\min}, \delta_{\min}+1, \ldots, \delta_{\max}\}$,
forward-simulating the CTM $T$ steps per candidate,
\begin{equation}
  \delta^* = \arg\min_{\delta \in \mathcal{G}} Z_{\text{CTM}}(\delta),
  \label{eq:cellsearch_argmin}
\end{equation}
accumulating bus-queue delay and side delay on the simulated cells
($|\mathcal{G}| \times T / \Delta t \sim$1000 cell-updates per detection,
feasible at typical headways).  The executable loop, SELFORG/balance
warrants, and commit path are stated in Alg.~\ref{alg:ctmgs}.  The method is
deterministic and unlearned: no training, no exploration, immediate
convergence.  Its failure mode is the local scope of the score: because
Eq.~\eqref{eq:cellsearch_argmin} prices only the cells it simulates, and the
commit is the $Z_1$ layer of Eq.~\eqref{eq:net_pick} on that score, the
downstream $Z_2$/cascade cost stays invisible---the $+49.3\%$ gridlock.
CellQLearn shares the CTM state but learns $\arg\max Q$ instead of
enumerating $\delta$; \cref{tab:methods} contrasts the two rows.

\paragraph{Objective mapping (Type~A).} CellSearch \emph{proposes} by the
exhaustive CTM simulation of Eq.~\eqref{eq:cellsearch_argmin} and
\emph{commits} through the $Z_1$ layer $\arg\min\Delta_{\text{net}}$ s.t.\ the
$Z_4$ veto (Alg.~\ref{alg:ctmgs}); Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives} places it in the
$Z_1$-net column.



% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Method IV: CTM Q-Learning (CellQLearn)}
\label{sec:sol_cellqlearn}
% ----------------------------------------------------

CellQLearn combines tabular Q-learning with CTM-computed rewards, following
\citep{Chanloha2014CellPriority}.  The state space uses CTM-based bins
($4 \times 4 \times 2 \times 4 = 128$ states): CTM bus queue, cross-traffic
pressure ratio, bus-phase indicator, and bus ETA.  The reward is the
CTM-estimated red-light delay decomposed into three \emph{local proxies} (not
the exact $Z$'s: $Z_2^{\text{CTM}}$ here is single-cell cross delay, not
green-wave bandwidth),
\begin{equation}
  R(a) = \alpha Z_1^{\text{CTM}}(a) + \beta Z_2^{\text{CTM}}(a) + \gamma Z_3^{\text{CTM}}(a),
  \label{eq:cellq_reward}
\end{equation}
warm-started from initial CTM estimates and updated by standard Q-learning.

The family spans four scored arms whose differences lie entirely in the
commit and gate, not the learner (\cref{tab:methods}): the un-gated core
proposes and commits by $\varepsilon$-greedy $\arg\max Q$, a Type~E step
protected only by the analytic cost veto, hence $+4.7\%$;
\texttt{CELLQLEARN\_GATED} keeps the same learner but re-commits through the
$Z_1$ layer ($\arg\min\Delta_{\text{net}}$ s.t.\ the hard balance gate
Eq.~\eqref{eq:rule_balance} and the $Z_4$ veto), and the 10.2-point swing
is that commit change, not the learner; \texttt{\_SAFE} and \texttt{\_FORCED}
vary the veto, warrant, and bus weighting.  $Z_3$ enters via
MIN\_GAIN/takeover and $Z_2$ via the cascade/Purdue terms, so
Fig.~\ref{fig:objectives} shows the family spanning the learned-proposer row
with a Type~A commit arrow for the gated variant.

% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Net-Benefit Gate and Projected-Saturation Cascade Cost}
\label{sec:gate_cascade}
% ----------------------------------------------------

The single-cell CTM reward prices the delay an action imposes on the
\emph{immediate} cross approach, but corridor gridlock typically forms two to
three junctions \emph{downstream}, where the released platoon meets a link
that cannot absorb it.  Two mechanisms make that network cost visible.

\emph{Measured cross cost.}  The cross-street cost of holding a movement on
red is priced from the measured cross-approach state---standing queue plus
arrivals over the extra red---inflated by the superlinear severity
Eq.~\eqref{eq:ctm_severity} as the measured saturation approaches jam; the
larger of the measured and analytic shockwave costs is used, so a congested
cross street is never under-priced.

\emph{Projected-saturation cascade.}  The released platoon is propagated
hop-by-hop along the bus route by
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:ctm_proj}--\eqref{eq:ctm_carry}, and the cost
$C^{\text{cas}}_a$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:ctm_cascade} is charged on the
\emph{projected} saturation after the platoon and the red-time arrivals land,
not on the still-moderate current queue, so gridlock that forms \emph{from}
the release is priced.  The gated commit then applies the balance rule
\cref{eq:rule_balance} in its estimated-state form, admitting $a$ only when
the total-passenger bus benefit $\text{bps}(a)$ (no transit-priority
multiplier) exceeds the measured-plus-cascade cross cost $\text{cpc}(a)$;
otherwise the controller holds.  The
un-gated core lacks this hard gate---its cost veto is a ratio test
$\text{cpc} > \theta\,\text{bps}$---which is why it can commit seed-dependent
over-actions that \texttt{\_GATED} suppresses.  The same measured cost and
cascade enter the Nash controller's individual-rationality test
(Sec.~\ref{sec:sol_nash}).

% ----------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{CellQLearn-v2: A Dense Corridor-Demand MDP on the Cell-Transition Model}
\label{sec:cellqlearn_v2}
% ----------------------------------------------------

The learner of Sec.~\ref{sec:sol_cellqlearn} is bus-triggered: three of its
four state dimensions (CTM bus queue, cross-ratio-versus-bus, bus ETA) are
defined \emph{relative to a detected bus}, so between buses the state collapses
to a degenerate cell and the Q-rows over its $128$ mostly bus-insertion actions
stay near zero -- the table is sparse and the policy defaults to \texttt{NA}.
CellQLearn-v2 re-poses the same tabular-Q idea as a \emph{continuous,
demand-driven} control of the running phase: at each decision tick it asks only
whether changing the \emph{current} phase improves corridor demand discharge on
the cell-transition model, and it does so with or without a bus present.  It is
one instance of the trigger-agnostic operation of Sec.~\ref{sec:bus_optional},
specialised to the extend/hold/truncate family and learned per junction.
It is a design variant, not one of the scored arms of
\cref{tab:methods}; its executable form is Alg.~\ref{alg:cellqlearn_v2}.

\paragraph{Per-junction MDP.} For each junction $i \in \Isp$ CellQLearn-v2 is an
independent (MARL-style, Sec.~\ref{sec:sol_marl_mp}) finite MDP
$\langle \Scal_i, \Acal_i, P^{\text{CTM}}, R^{\text{c}}, \gamma \rangle$, all
symbols in this subsubsection local to it.  The state (Fig.~\ref{fig:cq2_state})
is the dense, always-observable seven-tuple
\begin{equation}
  s_{i,t} = \bigl(\underbrace{\phi^{\text{cur}}_{i,t},\; \phi^{\text{cross}}_{i,t},\; \sigma^{\text{down}}_{i,t},\; \tau^{\text{phase}}_{i,t},\; \beta^{\text{bus}}_{i,t}}_{\text{local to junction }i},\; \underbrace{\sigma^{\text{ngh}}_{i,t},\; g^{\text{ngh}}_{i,t}}_{\text{corridor neighbours}}\bigr) \in \Scal_i,
  \label{eq:cq2_state}
\end{equation}
with the five local components quantised into three bins each and the two
neighbour components into three and two
($|\Scal_i| = 3^5 \times 3 \times 2 = 1458$).  The \emph{local} block is:
$\phi^{\text{cur}}_{i,t}$ the pax-weighted pressure on the phase currently green
and $\phi^{\text{cross}}_{i,t}$ the largest pressure on a conflicting phase
(both from the max-pressure pax weighting
$\mathcal{P}_{i,p,t}=\sum_{v}\rho_v Q_{i,p,v,t}$ of Sec.~\ref{sec:sol_marl_mp}),
$\sigma^{\text{down}}_{i,t}$ the downstream CTM saturation (the spillback-risk
entry the cascade of Sec.~\ref{sec:gate_cascade} prices),
$\tau^{\text{phase}}_{i,t}$ the elapsed green normalised by the
$[G^{\min}_i,G^{\max}_i]$ bounds, and $\beta^{\text{bus}}_{i,t}$ a bus-urgency
bin that is simply its lowest level (``no bus'') when
$b^{\text{present}}_{i,t}=0$.  The \emph{neighbour} block makes the policy
condition on the coordination relationship rather than a lone junction --- the
most consequential part of a corridor controller: $\sigma^{\text{ngh}}_{i,t} =
\max_{j \in \Ncal(i)} \sigma^{\text{down}}_{j,t}$ is the worst saturation over the
adjacent corridor junctions $\Ncal(i)$ (so a platoon is not pushed into a
neighbour that will spill it back), and $g^{\text{ngh}}_{i,t} =
\mathbb{1}[\exists\, j \in \Ncal(i):\, \text{$j$ green on its through phase}]$
flags that a downstream green-wave window is open.  Because every dimension is
pure demand/pressure/signal state, $s_{i,t}$ is informative every tick; the
bus-absence degeneracy of the v1 state is removed by construction.

